    "findings": [
      {
        "severity": "clarification",
        "summary": "Ownership-74 originally used three unrelated or giveaway alternatives rather than plausible supply-timing mistakes. Its first replacement also ambiguously described planning from earlier demand.",
        "resolution": "Author replaced the alternatives with resale versus new-production timing, premature counting of announced units, and immediate contraction of durable completed supply. The reviewer read the final revision 2 and checked the distinction against BOE AH501 printed pages 40-41."
      },
      {
        "severity": "clarification",
        "summary": "Ownership-79 used an invented one-month ownership prerequisite as a distractor.",
        "resolution": "Author replaced it with the neighboring five-year prescriptive-use requirement. The reviewer confirmed that prescription is a different doctrine and cannot supply missing prior common ownership for a necessity claim."
      },
      {
        "severity": "clarification",
        "summary": "Two DRE source locators were insufficiently precise for lien classification and encroachment discussions.",
        "resolution": "The lien-classification rule now includes ref05 printed page 61 for the explicit classification axes; the encroachment rule now identifies printed pages 76-77. Both passages were independently read."
      },
      {
        "severity": "clarification",
        "summary": "The community-instrument-joinder rule described the two-year lease example but did not link its concrete visual artifact.",
        "resolution": "Author added exp-ownership-community-two-year-lease to the example's figureIds. The reviewer inspected that existing specimen and confirmed its authority assumptions and source match."
      }
    ],
    "limitations": "Separate AI primary-source review, not human legal review or a guarantee of complete coverage or an exam result. Some primary passages were personally read during preceding ownership authorship and review passes and reused here; new and sensitive passages were checked directly. Older DRE reference chapters support stable concepts only; current statutes control mutable legal conditions. Murphy v. Burch was read as the court opinion reproduced by Stanford, not its third-party annotation. This record covers the first three ownership chapters and their specified 43 rules and 13 new items, not the other three ownership chapters, their new questions, held-out forms, or empirical question difficulty."
